      "text": "To solve this alternating sum problem, we notice that it follows an arithmetic rule: $\\{[\\text{(first number) }\\times 3] - [\\text{(third number) }\\times 2] + \\text{(last number)}\\} = \\text{(first sign number)}$. This pattern can be seen when we break down the given series into smaller subsets (also known as groups).\n\nApplying this rule, we observe that the total number of pairs (elements divisible by 3) in our series is 9. To simplify the expression, we calculate the product of every pair:\n\n$1 \\times 3 = 3$, $-4 \\times 2 = -8$, $7 \\times 3 = 21$, $-10 \\times 2 = -20$, $13 \\times 3 = 39$, $-46 \\times 2 = -92$, $49 \\times 3 = 147$, $-52 \\times 2 = -104$, $55 \\times 3 = 165$.\n\nAdding these products, we get the total sum:\n\n$3 - 8 + 21 - 20 + 39 - 92 + 147 - 104 + 165$.\n\nWe then remove the 165 from the total sum, resulting in:\n\n$3 + (-8) + 21 + (-20) + 39 + (-92) + 147 + (-104) + 165 - 165$.\n\nCombining like terms, we obtain:\n\n$3 + 21 + 39 + 147 + (-8) + (-20) + (-92) + (-104) + (-165) - 165 - 165 = 163 - 330 = \\boxed{-167}$.",
